WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.300
- Our next guest is a very funny man,

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:04.640
who took us on a virtual
tour of the Philippines,

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.020
when he was here last summer.

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:07.660
He's since become an author

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.360
and his book's called Mixed Plate.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.530
And it's out now, please welcome, Jo Koy.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:14.350
How did you come up with
the title of your book?

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:15.183
Mixed Plate?

00:00:15.183 --> 00:00:16.016
How did you come up with that?

00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:19.260
- Mixed Plate, it's just like, okay.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.080
One, I'm in love with Hawaii.
- Yeah.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:21.913
- You know what I mean?

00:00:21.913 --> 00:00:23.980
Hawaii really embraced me right away

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:25.620
when my first special came out

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.160
I broke a record in Hawaii.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.820
- [Kelly] Wow.
- I got my own day in Hawaii.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.590
I sold like 27,000 tickets

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:33.450
at this theater called
the Blaisdell theater.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.940
I broke Mariah Carey's record.
- What?

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.910
- Yeah, it was like the
most tickets ever sold.

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:41.020
And I gave it my own day and
I just love the culture there.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:42.185
I love the people there.

00:00:42.185 --> 00:00:46.120
And one of the things I love
to eat was the mixed plate.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:47.220
And when you look at the mixed plate,

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:50.631
it has everything like every
ethnicity is on the plate.

00:00:50.631 --> 00:00:51.600
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.400
- There's a little rice for Asians.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.000
There's Korean barbecue.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.365
There's tempura, Japanese.

00:00:56.365 --> 00:00:58.030
There's spam on it.

00:00:58.030 --> 00:00:59.480
There's mac salad.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.390
So when you look at that
plate, I think of me.

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:03.310
It's like a little bit

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:05.210
of all these cultures
just mixed up into me.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:07.860
And I just felt like that's
a good representation

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.180
of what this book is.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:11.950
And about my life in this world.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:14.500
- That's a beautiful way
to look at the world.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.470
- Yeah, 'cause you put all
that stuff on one plate

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:19.450
and it just becomes this
beautiful plate of food.

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:20.570
You know what I mean?
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:21.403
- And I feel like that's
what it took to make-

00:01:21.403 --> 00:01:23.130
- But I just mean, it's society.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:25.010
It's cool to look at the world like that.

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:25.880
Like we all count.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:26.713
We all matter.

00:01:26.713 --> 00:01:27.546
We all make the plate beautiful.

00:01:27.546 --> 00:01:29.290
- Yeah, you put it all-
- Even spam.

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:31.037
Even redneck spam.
- Even spam.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:33.640
You put spam next to a flaming yawn.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:35.450
You know what I'm eating that spam.

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:37.790
- [Kelly] Fine, it's fancier.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:39.190
Tastes better already.
- Right?

00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:40.623
- Well, you're candid
about your dad in the book.

00:01:40.623 --> 00:01:41.456
- Yeah.

00:01:41.456 --> 00:01:44.730
- So how did that relationship evolve?

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:47.500
- You know, we, it was at
the beginning, you know,

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:49.530
it was tough, you know,
product of divorce.

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:50.363
You know what I mean?

00:01:50.363 --> 00:01:54.690
And you know, I've never really
talked about it publicly,

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:56.306
you know what I mean?
- Yeah.

00:01:56.306 --> 00:01:57.740
- And how I struggled with identity.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:01:59.590
You know my dad's white,
my mom's Filipino.

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:01.100
My dad and mom divorced.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.920
And here I am being, you know,

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:06.450
with my mom pretty much
my whole teenage years

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:08.300
being raised by my mom.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:11.970
- And I never really had that
relationship with my father,

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.170
but when we rekindled it was hard.

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:15.010
I mean, it was good.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:16.290
It was hard in a good way.

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:18.469
And now we're just like
the best of friends.

00:02:18.469 --> 00:02:20.600
But it's, you know-
- Yeah.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:21.690
- When people see me go up there

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:24.480
and talk about my mom a lot,
it's like, that was my story.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:25.313
You know what I mean?
- Yeah.

00:02:25.313 --> 00:02:26.146
- That was my childhood.

00:02:26.146 --> 00:02:26.979
Those are my stories.

00:02:26.979 --> 00:02:30.610
And then, and now I'm building
this story with my father

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:31.443
and it's great.

00:02:31.443 --> 00:02:33.640
- I think that's really
important 'cause it's so funny,

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:38.170
'cause I know I'm from
two divorces with my mom

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:41.280
and it's one of those things
that I find in my experience,

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.320
not that this is a statistic by any means,

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:46.170
but that women are more apt
and open to talk about it

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:47.073
rather than men.
- Yeah.

00:02:47.073 --> 00:02:49.680
- So I think it's actually
really cool that you own that.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.760
Like something felt like it was missing

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:53.673
and you wanted that
relationship and that's okay.

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:54.510
- Yeah.
- You know?

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:56.830
- And you know what,
another thing, you know,

00:02:56.830 --> 00:03:00.400
I'm from a different generation
when divorce was still new

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.810
and you know, and my dad had to like,

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:04.130
he stepped away and whatever it is

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:05.640
that he did, he did, right?

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.620
But I think what I learned from it was

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.120
I don't ever want my son
to feel that feeling.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:11.953
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:11.953 --> 00:03:14.680
- And I know that divorce is hard-

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:16.120
- [Kelly] Yes.
- But you can make it work.

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:17.190
We're still best friends.

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:18.600
Me and my ex-
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:19.567
- Were the best of friends.
- [Kelly] That's beautiful.

00:03:19.567 --> 00:03:21.440
- She has all the codes to my house.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:23.010
She has the keys to my front door.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:24.980
She can walk in whenever she wants.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:26.560
- Wow.
- We are the best of friends.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:28.830
When we go on vacation, she comes.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:31.690
Whatever she has at her
house is in my house.

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:33.576
Like my son never feels this difference

00:03:33.576 --> 00:03:34.540
between the two.

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:36.470
We're a loving family

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:38.720
and we can work.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:39.553
You know what I mean?

00:03:39.553 --> 00:03:42.450
Like, just because you're not married,

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:44.530
it doesn't mean you can't
be the best of friends.

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:45.620
You still have the responsibility

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:47.420
to raise this beautiful child together.

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:50.540
And it's fun to see my
son see his parents happy.

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:51.373
- Absolutely.

00:03:51.373 --> 00:03:52.206
- Oh, she gets to the world.

00:03:52.206 --> 00:03:53.039
She lives right next to me.

00:03:53.039 --> 00:03:53.890
Everywhere I move.

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:55.350
She moves next to me, so.

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:56.610
- Wow.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:03:56.610 --> 00:04:00.610
- I got to say you and Gwen
know how to nail a divorce.

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:04.610
You know how to really do it
and be successful afterwards.

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.130
- Yeah, it's not hard though, right?

00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:07.470
- Yeah.
- You got great kids-

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:08.650
- It is hard-
- It is.

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:10.480
- coming from experience currently.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:11.400
It's hard.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.780
- I know, but I think sometimes
you just got to step back

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:16.190
and just look at those kids
and just go, you know what?

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:17.023
It's for them.

00:04:17.023 --> 00:04:18.350
What are all suffering?
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:19.237
- [Jo Koy] Whatever.
- No, that's the easy part.

00:04:19.237 --> 00:04:20.070
- [Jo Koy] Yeah.

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:20.903
- I think that's the easy part.

00:04:20.903 --> 00:04:21.736
- [Jo Koy] Yeah.

00:04:21.736 --> 00:04:24.390
Well, everybody, Joe's
book is called Mixed Plate.

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:25.590
Be sure to check it out.

